  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Discovery / Cloud Service Discovery", "technique": "T1526", "what_happened": "At 2026-04-11 01:02:18 CEST, Wazuh's GCP module logged a burst of Cloud Audit Log events tied to identity enumeration. data.srcip=10.108.57.184 issued gcloud auth list, gcloud config list and a gcloud projects describe call against project corp-prod-987654. The projects describe call returned AccessDenied but the Cloud Audit Log still recorded the caller identity (service account compute@developer.gserviceaccount.com). Wazuh fired a GCP rule referencing 'IAM identity enumeration / projects.describe by unexpected principal'. This is the classic reconnaissance step that precedes resource abuse: confirm which identity is in hand and which project it can reach before attempting any action.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'gcp' and rule.description matched 'projects.describe' / 'IAM identity enumeration' with data.srcip=10.108.57.184. The univariate signature: a single source driving auth/config/projects API calls against project corp-prod-987654 with an AccessDenied response still logged by Cloud Audit Logs.", "first_seen": "2026-04-11T01:02:18+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Discovery / Cloud Service Discovery — Pub/Sub topic enumeration", "technique": "T1526", "what_happened": "At 01:05:30 CEST (~3 minutes after the identity recon), the same data.srcip=10.108.57.184 issued gcloud pubsub topics list and gcloud pubsub subscriptions list against project corp-prod-987654. Cloud Audit Logs recorded pubsub.topics.list and pubsub.subscriptions.list calls by service account compute@developer.gserviceaccount.com. Wazuh fired a GCP rule referencing 'Pub/Sub topic enumeration by unexpected principal'. The attacker is mapping the message-bus topology — specifically which topics exist and which subscriptions (and therefore which Cloud Functions) are wired to them — to find a topic whose publish action will trigger code execution.", "chain_reason": "data.srcip=10.108.57.184 generated pubsub.topics.list + pubsub.subscriptions.list Cloud Audit Log events on project corp-prod-987654 by the same principal compute@developer.gserviceaccount.com seen in step 1. rule.groups contained 'gcp' and rule.description matched 'Pub/Sub topic enumeration'. Topic-then-subscription enumeration is the canonical precursor to a Pub/Sub-triggered Cloud Function abuse.", "first_seen": "2026-04-11T01:05:30+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Execution / Serverless Execution — Pub/Sub publish triggers Cloud Function", "technique": "T1648", "what_happened": "At 01:08:12 CEST, data.srcip=10.108.57.184 issued gcloud pubsub topics publish against topic projects/corp-prod-987654/topics/evt-bus-prod with a crafted message payload {\"exec\":\"id\",\"c2\":\"10.108.57.184\",...} and attributes source=attacker,attacker_ip=10.108.57.184. Cloud Audit Logs recorded pubsub.topics.publish to the sensitive production topic evt-bus-prod. Because evt-bus-prod has a subscription bound to a Cloud Function, the publish triggers Cloud Function execution WITHOUT a direct invoke call — Serverless Execution (T1648). Wazuh fired rule 92974 'GCP Pub/Sub publish to sensitive topic'. The attacker also attempted a REST API publish directly against pubsub.googleapis.com to bypass gcloud SDK restrictions.", "chain_reason": "data.srcip=10.108.57.184 generated a pubsub.topics.publish Cloud Audit Log event targeting the production topic evt-bus-prod, immediately after the topic enumeration in step 2. rule.id 92974 fired and rule.description matched 'Pub/Sub publish to sensitive topic' with rule.mitre.id T1648. Publishing to a function-bound topic is indirect code execution — the defining property of Serverless Execution.", "first_seen": "2026-04-11T01:08:12+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Execution / Serverless Execution — rogue Cloud Function deployment", "technique": "T1648", "what_happened": "From 01:10:05 to 01:12:40 CEST, data.srcip=10.108.57.184 issued gcloud functions deploy for two functions — data-helper-<ts> (--trigger-http --allow-unauthenticated, entry-point handler, service account compute@developer.gserviceaccount.com, env var C2=10.108.57.184) and data-helper-<ts>-pubsub (--trigger-topic evt-bus-prod). Cloud Audit Logs recorded cloudfunctions.CreateFunction calls by the same principal; the deploy attempts returned AccessDenied but the attempt was still recorded. In parallel Wazuh File Integrity Monitoring flagged the dropped function source at /tmp/func_<ts>/main.py — a Python stub that queries the GCE metadata endpoint http://metadata.google.internal/computeMetadata/v1/instance/service-accounts/default/token and POSTs the token to the attacker C2. Wazuh fired rule 92975 'GCP Cloud Function deployment by unexpected account'. Deploying an --allow-unauthenticated HTTP function plus a Pub/Sub-triggered function is an attempt to establish persistent serverless code execution that survives credential rotation.", "chain_reason": "data.srcip=10.108.57.184 generated cloudfunctions.CreateFunction Cloud Audit Log events and Wazuh FIM (syscheck) flagged /tmp/func_<ts>/main.py on the same host. rule.id 92975 fired and rule.description matched 'Cloud Function deployment by unexpected account' with rule.mitre.id T1648. The function source's GCE metadata-token theft + C2 POST is the canonical serverless-persistence-and-token-exfil signature.", "first_seen": "2026-04-11T01:10:05+0200"}
  ],
